 :findings [{:id :F1 :class :missing-concrete-algorithm :location "plan lines 24-25, 40"
             :issue "Full transitive package and test closure is demanded without defining a bounded, static, conservative inventory or how unknown dynamic/build-constraint cases are represented. Exact consumption cannot be promised from an unexecuted Go toolchain, and a whole-tree pin does not itself identify package closure."
             :required "Specify source-only traversal from both target packages: inventory module/workspace metadata and replacements; regular files, symlinks, test/helper files, imports, embeds, generated inputs and applicable build constraints for the fixed GOOS/GOARCH/CGO=0 profile; follow local and selected module imports conservatively, explicitly include standard library/toolchain inputs, and record ambiguity, missing paths, unsafe symlinks and unsupported constructs as unresolved. Bound traversal by fixed roots, bytes, entries and time chosen and reviewed before inventory; fail closed on exhaustion. Pin a conservative superset with path/type/mode/content and resolve containment for symlinks; no Go command, app execution or runtime discovery is a prerequisite to source authoring. Exact values for those inventory ceilings are required before that inventory is admitted, not invented as host observations here."}
            {:id :F2 :class :missing-concrete-algorithm :location "plan lines 27-29, 41"
             :issue "The requested exact executable allow policy and inherited metadata/escape denials are stated as outcomes, without an enforceable mechanism or a Go child-exec transition model. Seccomp syscall filtering alone cannot compare executable path strings or safely establish executable identity; read-only mounts alone do not supply a pathname execution allowlist."
             :required "Author and independently review an actual kernel-enforced identity-bound executable authorization mechanism, including execve/execveat, scripts/interpreters, dynamic loaders, file descriptors, alternate ABIs, compiler/assembler/linker subprocesses, process/thread inheritance and substitution races. Design a minimal fixed pinned executable set and a static candidate tool invocation/child model, treating unknown legitimate Go subprocesses as unresolved rather than widening access. Specify NNP and seccomp architecture and denied networking, namespace/mount, ptrace, metadata and escape syscalls, including io_uring and inherited descriptors; specify the minimal proc/dev view and remaining channels. If a mechanism cannot be established, compile remains blocked; a future neutral probe may determine feasibility, not authorize or run Go."}
            {:id :F3 :class :missing-concrete-algorithm :location "plan lines 29-33"
             :issue "The aggregate and cache hard limits and lifecycle are requirements, not a selected implementable storage/accounting design. In particular, a nested cache limit and retained logs must have defined quota, inode/metadata/link, output and shutdown semantics; capacity polling is not enforcement."
             :required "Specify a preauthorized kernel-enforced aggregate 512 MiB store and independent 256 MiB cache sublimit, inode/metadata/link accounting, placement of every writable path and bounded retained controller output, and behavior at byte/inode/quota exhaustion. Prove how the aggregate includes any nested backing store rather than double-counting or bypassing it; distinguish kernel backing capacity from cgroup memory and validate both. Source the controller's pre-release cgroup verification, shared deadlines, output truncation/overflow handling, descendant termination, reaping and original-cgroup emptiness evidence from the neutral design with changed code independently reviewed. Unknown quota, mount, cgroup or user-namespace capability goes to the single separately admitted neutral feasibility probe; failure or unavailable proof blocks compile, not source authoring."}
            {:id :F4 :class :preservation-contradiction :location "plan lines 26, 29, 33, 43"
             :issue "Retain all artifacts/no cleaner and prove cgroup shutdown do not define how ephemeral writable stores survive mount teardown or process exit. A tmpfs-backed sandbox can destroy unique bytes implicitly, including build intermediates that Go itself normally removes; mere postflight inventory cannot recover already deleted files."
             :required "Before selecting ephemeral storage, specify all generated and copied artifact classes, where they live, and exact lifecycle through Go's own temporary-file behavior, quota failure, timeout, forced termination, supervisor crash and host shutdown. Establish a reviewed durable retention/recoverability mechanism for every created artifact or copy before it can disappear, with identity, completeness and verified recoverable backing, and explicit authorization for any destruction; if normal Go cleanup can remove unrecovered unique bytes, select a different reviewed mechanism or block that compile design. Account for volatile mount teardown independently of process cleanup. No implicit exemption for tool-internal cleanup or for scratch. Preserve probe evidence under the same user preservation rule; an unproven retention mechanism is a blocker to that storage design, not proof all designs are impossible."}]
